# Copy to .env.local at the repository root. .env.local is gitignored; a real
# environment variable beats it, which is what makes CI and launchd work with
# no file at all.
#
# **Every name here begins LINGTAI_** (#63), and the reason is not tidiness.
# A managed project's own environment file is merged over this one, so a project
# whose file was missing a DATABASE_URL line used to get *Lingtai's own log*
# under a name its application connects to without hesitating — and the recipe's
# `env.required` could not tell that apart from a correct line, because the
# merged data had a value either way. No application asks for LINGTAI_*, so a
# missing line is now an absent value, which refuses the run loudly.
#
# A project's own file keeps its own names: DATABASE_URL there stays
# DATABASE_URL, because that is what the project's application reads.

# --- the event log ----------------------------------------------------------
# One database, and on a plain Postgres one line (#176):
LINGTAI_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres
#
# The second line is needed only when the first goes through a transaction
# pooler — Supabase's `...pooler.supabase.com`, PgBouncer, port 6543. Migrations
# and LISTEN/NOTIFY need session mode, and a transaction pooler breaks both
# *without erroring* — a cross-connection NOTIFY simply never
# arrives. See doc/decisions/0009-two-connections.md.
#
# Unset, LINGTAI_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL is LINGTAI_DATABASE_URL; set, it wins.
# `lingtai doctor` refuses a pooled URL standing in for it.
#LINGTAI_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres
#
# This must be its own database, not one belonging to a managed project.
# Lingtai has to keep running while a managed project is the thing being
# changed.

# --- the log the tests write to --------------------------------------------
# A *different* database, and the suite refuses to run without it.
#
# The tests are not mocked: they append real events, run real projections.
# Pointed at the operator's own log, they leave work
# items and board cards behind — twenty-four cards from ten throwaway `esctest*`
# projects, in the run that led to this section existing.
#
# Cleaning that up is not cheap either. Truncating a projection and replaying it
# brings the cards straight back, so the only way to remove them is to delete
# from an append-only table, which this system should never make routine.
#
# Give it its schema with:
#   LINGTAI_TEST=1 pnpm db:init && LINGTAI_TEST=1 pnpm db:bootstrap
#
# **Neither string may go through a transaction pooler** (`#157`). Both of these
# pointed at `aws-0-us-east-1.pooler.supabase.com` for months, and the suite
# failed at a rate that made a red gate mean nothing: `run-once.test.ts` went
# `30 passed → 10 failed → 31 passed` on one commit inside an hour, and every
# failure was a dropped or timed-out connection rather than an assertion. Four
# tickets were refused by `build` in a single day and none of them by their own
# code. Pointed at the direct host instead, the same suite ran ten times in a
# row green, 480–502s.
#
# On Supabase the direct host is `db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432`; the pooler
# is the `...pooler.supabase.com` one, and it is what the dashboard offers
# first. The two strings below are the same database either way — what differs
# is whether a pooler sits in front of it. The TEST_ pair falls back the same
# way as the pair above, and only within itself: with no
# LINGTAI_TEST_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL, LINGTAI_TEST_DATABASE_URL stands in.
#
# `?pgbouncer=true` belongs only on the pooled string. `lingtai doctor` refuses
# it on a direct connection, and is right to: it is a claim about a pooler that
# is not there.
#
# Note for a machine that is not this one: Supabase's direct host resolves to
# IPv6 only unless the IPv4 add-on is bought. It works here; it may not work
# from CI.
LINGTAI_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@db.your-project-ref.supabase.co:5432/postgres?pgbouncer=true
LINGTAI_TEST_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:password@db.your-project-ref.supabase.co:5432/postgres

# --- GitHub -----------------------------------------------------------------
# An App, not a personal access token: a fine-grained token can be wrong in a
# way nothing reports. See README "Connecting GitHub" and
# doc/decisions/0006-github-app.md.
#
# The three names below are written for you by the board's /setup/github-app
# page, which creates the App from a manifest in one click (#169) — into
# .env.local, not this file. Fill them in by hand only if you created the App
# yourself (doc/operating.md "Connecting GitHub").
LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The private key. A path by default so the key never has to be pasted into a
# shell; `~` is expanded. LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY takes the PEM inline instead,
# for hosts that can only carry it as one line.
LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=~/.ssh/lingtai-agent.private-key.pem
#LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional. Set the same value in the GitHub App's webhook settings, point the
# App at <your tunnel>/api/webhook, and a new issue reaches the daemon in
# seconds instead of waiting for its sweep.
#
# Leaving it unset is fine: the daemon sweeps GitHub on a long interval anyway,
# so a webhook is an optimisation and never a dependency. Without it the route
# answers 503 rather than pretending a delivery landed.
LINGTAI_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
